  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "A Minnesota river splits in two and half completely vanishes. At Devil's Kettle falls, the right side flows downstream, but the left side roars straight into a deep stone pothole.",
        "visual_query": "brule river minnesota waterfalls rocks",
        "visual_fallback": "rushing waterfall rapids"
      },
      {
        "narration": "For decades, researchers tried tracing where it went by dumping bright fluorescent dye, logs, and hundreds of ping-pong balls directly into the chasm.",
        "visual_query": "minnesota state park river canyon",
        "visual_fallback": "swirling water vortex"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Not a single ball or trace of dye ever emerged downstream. How do millions of gallons of water vanish into thin air?",
        "visual_query": "lake superior minnesota rocky shoreline",
        "visual_fallback": "deep river pool"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Wild theories blamed bottomless tectonic faults, until state hydrologists finally measured the river's water volume above and below the falls in 2017.",
        "visual_query": "hydrologist measuring river water flow",
        "visual_fallback": "scientist measuring river"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Both flow readings were identical. The water wasn't vanishing into a subterranean abyss\u2014powerful underwater currents simply force it through hidden fractures right back into the river pool below.",
        "visual_query": "devils kettle waterfall rock formation",
        "visual_fallback": "underwater river current"
      }
    ],
